   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Soaking gives beans time to take up water, which shortens cooking. Hard water can stop beans softening. Above 2,000 feet (about 600 m), add 5% to the cooking time for every 1,000 feet (about 300 m); at 7,000 feet it may take 25% longer. A pressure cooker speeds things up, but its times need adjusting."
   },
   "source_url": "https://extension.colostate.edu/resource/cooking-with-dry-beans-and-other-pulses/",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  },
  {
   "id": "salt-late",
   "title": {
    "en": "Salt and acid in beans"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Salt shortens cooking time and helps beans keep their shape. Add acidic ingredients such as tomato in large amounts only at the end, once the beans are soft."
   },
   "source_url": "https://extension.colostate.edu/resource/cooking-with-dry-beans-and-other-pulses/",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  },
  {
   "id": "refry-beans",
   "title": {
    "en": "Fry and mash cooked beans"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Heat oil in a skillet over medium heat. Add the cooked beans about 1/2 cup at a time and mash with the back of a spoon. If the mix is thick, add about 1 tablespoon of the saved cooking liquid. Keep going until it is the texture you want, then stir in salt."
   },
   "source_url": "https://reallifegoodfood.umn.edu/side-dishes/refried-beans",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  },
  {
   "id": "masa-shaping",
   "title": {
    "en": "Shape pupusas from masa"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Mix masa harina with salt, add warm water slowly and stir until it thickens, then knead until smooth. Divide into balls. Flatten a ball in your palm, put a heaping tablespoon of filling in the middle, wrap the dough around it and flatten. Cook in a skillet over medium heat, 4 to 6 minutes per side, until lightly browned."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.umass.edu/agriculture-food-environment/nutrition/publications-resources/recipes/bean-cheese-pupusas",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  },
  {
   "id": "beans-vitamin-c",
   "title": {
    "en": "Pair beans with vitamin C"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Vitamin C eaten at the same meal can improve absorption of iron from plant foods. Add tomato, chili, cilantro or fruit to a bean meal."
   },
   "source_url": "https://nutritionsource.hsph.harvard.edu/iron/",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  }
 ],
 "safety": [
  {
   "id": "red-flags",
   "title": {
    "en": "Get help now"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Contact your mission nurse now if you have bloody diarrhea, a fever over 102F (about 39C), vomiting so often you cannot keep liquids down, or signs of dehydration such as passing very little urine, a dry mouth and throat, or feeling dizzy when you stand up. Also contact them if diarrhea lasts more than 3 days."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/signs-symptoms/index.html",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29",
   "urgent": true
  },
  {
   "id": "water-boil",
   "title": {
    "en": "Make water safe"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Follow your mission's water guidance first. If you need to treat water, bring it to a rolling boil for 1 minute. Above 6,500 feet (about 2,000 m), such as Mexico City, boil it for 3 minutes."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.cdc.gov/water-emergency/about/",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  },
  {
   "id": "water-altitude",
   "title": {
    "en": "Boiling at high altitude"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Above 6,500 feet (about 2,000 m), boil for 3 minutes."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.cdc.gov/water-emergency/about/",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29",
   "regions": [
    "mx-central"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "altitude-illness",
   "title": {
    "en": "Altitude sickness"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Headache with dizziness, tiredness or nausea in the first hours after arriving high up can be altitude sickness. Do not go higher while you have symptoms. If they get worse despite rest, go lower and tell your mission nurse."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.cdc.gov/yellow-book/hcp/environmental-hazards-risks/high-altitude-travel-and-altitude-illness.html",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29",
   "regions": [
    "mx-central"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "produce-wash",
   "title": {
    "en": "Wash produce"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Rinse fruit and vegetables under running water before eating or cooking."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/prevention/index.html",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  },
  {
   "id": "two-hour",
   "title": {
    "en": "The 2-hour rule"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Perishable food should not sit out more than 2 hours, or 1 hour if it is hotter than 90F (32C). Reheat leftovers to 165F (74C). A fridge should be 40F (4C) or colder."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/prevention/index.html",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  },
  {
   "id": "ors",
   "title": {
    "en": "Diarrhoea and dehydration"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Oral rehydration salts (ORS) treat diarrhoea. Tell your mission nurse if there is blood in the stool, or with sunken eyes, being unable to drink, unusual sleepiness, or skin that stays pinched. For how many days is too long, see the Get help now card."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/diarrhoeal-disease",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  },
  {
   "id": "heat",
   "title": {
    "en": "Heat illness"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Heat exhaustion: headache, nausea, dizziness, weakness, thirst, heavy sweating, passing less urine. Get out of the heat, drink liquids in frequent sips, cool down with cold water, and go to a clinic. Confusion, slurred speech, loss of consciousness or seizures can mean heat stroke: call emergency services."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/heat-stress/about/illnesses.html",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  },
  {
   "id": "five-keys",
   "title": {
    "en": "Five keys to safer food"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Keep clean. Separate raw and cooked. Cook thoroughly. Keep food at safe temperatures. Use safe water and raw materials."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241594639",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  },
  {
   "id": "raw-produce",
   "title": {
    "en": "Raw produce and cold storage"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Raw produce has been linked to illness, and Listeria can grow even in the cold."
   },
   "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/food-safety",
   "accessed": "2026-10-29"
  }
 ],
